For years, Swift was perceived mainly as a UI language tied to Apple client devices. With Swift 6 and strict concurrency checking, it has matured into a viable systems and cloud language, pairing Rust-like data-race safety with predictable, reference-counted performance. To support this ecosystem, Google engineering has launched the official Google Cloud API Client Libraries for Swift . Built from the ground up for Swift 6.2+, this new SDK uses the latest non-blocking Swift NIO event loops, HTTP/2 multiplexing, gRPC transport, and zero-cost compile-time data race safety. In this article, we'll walk you through all you need to know to get started, and to understand how the Server Side Cloud Swift SDK, or google-cloud-swift , works. The rise of server-side Swift and cloud-native concurrency Traditional backend development often forces a trade-off between developer ergonomics and resource utilization. While managed runtimes offer rapid development, lower-level systems languages provide finer control over memory and CPU footprint, often at the expense of feature velocity. Server-side Swift aims to strike a practical balance. Swift pairs a lightweight runtime and Automatic Reference Counting (ARC) with expressive syntax. More importantly, Swift 6 introduces compile-time concurrency checking. When you share state between async tasks across a cloud microservice, the compiler enforces that types conform to Sendable . Data races are caught in your editor before a binary ever compiles or reaches production. At the network layer, every request to Google Cloud APIs runs over event-driven, non-blocking sockets that scale across multicore Linux server environments without spawning system threads per connection. Where to use the Swift SDK The Server Side Cloud Swift SDK is engineered for server, container, and automated DevOps environments. When you build high-throughput microservices with Swift web frameworks like Hummingbird or Vapor , google-cloud-swift provides native access to Cloud Storage, AI, Identity and Access Management (IAM), and over one hundred other Google Cloud services. You can containerize your executable on Linux and deploy directly to Cloud Run , Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) , or Compute Engine VMs. Because the SDK compiles on macOS, and Linux, you can develop the backend in your preferred development environment, and then seamlessly deploy to production. And using Swift on both the frontend and backend allows you to share application-specific types across both. The SDK also excels at platform engineering and DevOps automation. You can author cross-platform CLI utilities and data rotation scripts that run on your developer laptop or inside CI/CD pipelines. These tools authenticate automatically against Google Cloud using Application Default Credentials (ADC) or Workload Identity Federation . If you're building an iOS , iPadOS , or visionOS app for the Apple App Store , you should not embed google-cloud-swift directly into your client bundle. Shipping Google Cloud service account keys or administrative credentials inside a client binary creates security risks. For direct client-side features, use the Firebase SDK for Apple Platforms to handle user authentication, real-time Firestore sync, and client-side security rules, or route requests through your own Cloud Run backend API. Getting started with your IDE and packages Because google-cloud-swift treats Linux and macOS as first-class citizens, you can develop on Apple hardware with Xcode or on Linux workstations with Visual Studio Code and swiftly . To install the official Swift compiler on Linux workstations using the swiftly CLI installer, run: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'curl -O https://download.swift.org/swiftly/linux/swiftly-$(uname -m).tar.gz &&
tar zxf swiftly-$(uname -m).tar.gz &&
./swiftly init --quiet-shell-followup &&
. "${SWIFTLY_HOME_DIR:-$HOME/.local/share/swiftly}/env.sh" &&
hash -r'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d5890>)])]> Alternatively, you can download prebuilt toolchain tarballs directly from official Swift Downloads for Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, or Amazon Linux. Note that google-cloud-swift requires Swift 6.2 or later , so verify your compiler version with swift --version after installation. To resolve Swift Package Manager bare repository trust warnings when cloning across Linux filesystems, configure Git before building with git config --global safe.bareRepository all . Add the required packages to your Package.swift manifest: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'swift package add-dependency https://github.com/googleapis/swift-google-cloud-language-v2.git --from 0.4.0
swift package add-target-dependency GoogleCloudLanguageV2 CloudBackendService --package swift-google-cloud-language-v2'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d4f50>)])]> On macOS you need to change the platforms directive: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', '// swift-tools-version: 6.2
import PackageDescription

let package = Package(
 name: "CloudBackendService",
 // Applied when compiling on Darwin/macOS; ignored by SPM on Linux targets
 platforms: [.macOS(.v15)],
 ... ...'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc411fbb190>)])]> In most environments a default-initialized client can make requests: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'import Foundation
import GoogleCloudLanguageV2

func analyzeTextSentiment(text: String) async throws {
 // Initialize explicit API key credentials
 let client = try LanguageServiceClient()

 // Configure request using structured builder closure
 let document = Document().with {
 $0.type = .plainText
 $0.source = .content(text)
 }

 let response = try await client.analyzeSentiment(
 request: AnalyzeSentimentRequest().with { $0.document = document }
 )

 if let sentiment = response.documentSentiment {
 print("Document sentiment score: \(sentiment.score)")
 }
}'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc411fb9210>)])]> Notice how Document().with { ... } avoids verbose temporary variables or mutating setters by providing a clean, thread-safe configuration closure. Networking, transport, and authentication The repository splits infrastructure primitives into modular packages under packages/ : swift-google-cloud-auth : Implements Application Default Credentials discovery, service account JWT signing, external account exchange for Workload Identity Federation, and API keys. swift-google-cloud-wkt : Provides idiomatic Swift types for Google Protocol Buffer well-known types, including nanosecond-precision Timestamp representations that bridge cleanly to Swift's Date . swift-google-cloud-gax : Handles Google API Extensions such as automated retry loops, exponential backoff, and pagination state machines. When you initialize any client library without arguments, Credentials.default() automatically scans your environment ( GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS , quota project variables, or the local Google Cloud CLI configuration) and authenticates connections over gRPC or HTTP/2. If you need to programmatically override credentials with an API key or attach custom access headers, you can pass explicit configuration options. For example, you could modify the previous example to use the following: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'import Foundation
import GoogleCloudAuth
import GoogleCloudGax
import GoogleCloudLanguageV2

func analyzeTextSentiment(apiKey: String, text: String) async throws {
 // Initialize explicit API key credentials
 let credentials = try Credentials(configuration: .apiKey(apiKey))
 let client = try LanguageServiceClient(
 ClientOptions().with { $0.credentials = credentials }
 )

 // Configure request using structured builder closure
 let document = Document().with {
 $0.type = .plainText
 $0.source = .content(text)
 }

 let response = try await client.analyzeSentiment(
 request: AnalyzeSentimentRequest().with { $0.document = document }
 )

 if let sentiment = response.documentSentiment {
 print("Document sentiment score: \(sentiment.score)")
 }
}'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc411fbb610>)])]> The autogenerated client ecosystem Google Cloud operates a vast ecosystem of APIs whose schemas update regularly. The teams supporting google-cloud-swift use code generators to automatically update the client libraries with the latest features and with new APIs. Using code generators produces stable APIs, without disruptive breaking changes. While the releases are on a fixed cadence, please contact Cloud Customer Care if you need a particular feature or API urgently. Whether you need to rotate keys in Secret Manager or invoke multimodal inference models via the Gemini API on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform , the generated SDKs follow consistent naming and async/await signatures. These generated clients offer more than plain unary RPC wrappers. They also offer wrappers that simplify application development. For example, iterating over long results involves fetching pages of results with one RPC, iterating over the page of results, and then preparing a new request to retrieve the following page. Using the generated clients this becomes an asynchronous iterator. This example shows how to query project secrets using GoogleCloudSecretManagerV1 : code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'import Foundation
import GoogleCloudSecretManagerV1

@main
struct SecretManagerQuickstart {
 static func main() async throws {
 guard let projectId = CommandLine.arguments.dropFirst().first else {
 print("Usage: SecretManagerQuickstart ")
 exit(1)
 }

 // Connects using Application Default Credentials automatically
 let client = try SecretManagerServiceClient()

 let request = ListSecretsRequest().with {
 $0.parent = "projects/\(projectId)"
 }

 // Async sequence streams pages of secrets automatically
 print("Secrets in project \(projectId):")
 for try await item in try client.listSecretsByItem(request: request) {
 print(" - \(item.name)")
 }
 }
}'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc411fba110>)])]> The pagination response returns an asynchronous sequence ( AsyncSequence ). You can iterate over items with for try await while the client library fetches subsequent pages in the background over non-blocking NIO channels. Where to go next With google-cloud-swift , server-side Swift developers can write end-to-end cloud infrastructure with compile-time race safety, native async/await ergonomic APIs, and zero OS thread congestion. To inspect the source code, open issues, or contribute new veneers, visit the official repository at googleapis/google-cloud-swift . Want to discuss server-side Swift architectures or Cloud Run containerization? Join the Google Developer Program to continue the conversation.

When we talk about agents, that doesn't necessarily mean we're talking about Python. In today's modern ecosystem, an agent might run as a high-concurrency Go microservice on Cloud Run , or live inside a browser tab calling Chrome's on-device model. Yet many agent frameworks assume a Python-only stack. That assumption forces teams to either spin up a Python service just for their agent workloads, or to build agent orchestration logic from scratch in their own language. Google's Agent Development Kit (ADK) removes that trade-off with coverage for Python , Go , TypeScript , Java , and Kotlin . Each version of the ADK is built from the ground up for its own runtime and type system, while sharing a unified architecture with interoperability through open protocols. A shared foundation Picking a language doesn't lock in your architecture: all five support the same open protocols. ADK includes a Model Context Protocol client in every language, so a custom tool exposed as an MCP server can be called from any of them immediately. For multi-agent systems, ADK supports the Agent2Agent (A2A) protocol, allowing an agent to communicate with a remote agent over HTTP. Through A2A, agents written in different languages or frameworks are interoperable. Starting agent-first To get started, run the one-time setup command to check your Google Cloud authentication, install the CLI, and then set up first-party ADK skills in coding agents like Antigravity , Claude Code , or Codex : code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'uvx google-agents-cli setup'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d56d0>)])]> Then, run the scaffold command to generate a working project with A2A endpoints and deployment config for Cloud Run, Gemini Enterprise Agent Runtime , or Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) : code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', '# Scaffold an ADK project (use --agent adk for Python or --agent adk_go for Go)
agents-cli scaffold create my-go-agent \
 --agent adk_go \
 --deployment-target cloud_run \
 --region us-central1'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d5c50>)])]> Both the ADK for Python and ADK for Kotlin repositories also ship agent builder skills in their source trees, and the adk.dev/llms.txt resource gives any coding agent on-demand API documentation across all five languages. Coding-agent skills scaffold your project while MCP and A2A connect all five language runtimes. Hands-on view of each ADK The clearest way to feel the difference between the five languages is to define the same weather tool in each one and see how its design fits the runtime. Python In ADK for Python , you don't need a decorator or a schema builder class. You pass a plain Python function into the tools parameter, and ADK inspects its signature, type hints, and docstring at startup to automatically construct the tool declaration and Pydantic parameter schema: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'from google.adk.agents.llm_agent import Agent


def get_weather(city: str) -> dict:
 """Retrieves the current weather report for a specified city.

 Args:
 city (str): The name of the city for which to retrieve the weather report.
 """
 return {"status": "success", "report": f"Sunny and 72F in {city}."}


root_agent = Agent(
 name="weather_agent",
 model="gemini-3.8-flash",
 instruction="Answer questions about the weather in a city.",
 tools=[get_weather],
)'), ('language', 'lang-py'), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d4990>)])]> Beyond fast prototyping, Python is ADK's reference implementation, shipping the broadest set of Google Cloud integrations ( BigQuery , Spanner , Bigtable ) and model adapters ( Gemini , Anthropic, OpenAI, LiteLLM). Paired with the adk eval or agents-cli eval run commands , its evaluation tooling scores agent trajectories against golden datasets in continuous integration (CI) to test the whole system end to end, even when your production service runs in another language. Go While Python leads in experimentation and evaluation, ADK for Go hits the sweet spot for production backend engineering. A Go tool function takes the agent.Context configuration and a strongly typed input struct. The functiontool.New function uses Go generics to infer both the input and output schemas from your struct tags: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'type GetWeatherArgs struct {
	City string `json:"city" jsonschema:"The city to look up weather for."`
}

type GetWeatherResult struct {
	Report string `json:"report"`
}

func getWeather(_ agent.Context, args GetWeatherArgs) (GetWeatherResult, error) {
	return GetWeatherResult{Report: "Sunny and 72F in " + args.City}, nil
}

weatherTool, err := functiontool.New(functiontool.Config{
	Name: "get_weather",
	Description: "Retrieves the current weather report for a city.",
}, getWeather)'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d43d0>)])]> Because the functiontool.New function is generic over your handler's signature, the Go compiler verifies that your function matches the expected contract before your binary ever runs. Go was also the first language after Python to ship the ADK 2.0 Graph Workflow runtime . When you need deterministic control flow alongside LLM reasoning, the workflow.New function wires agents and plain Go functions into a directed graph with conditional routing and parallel steps: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'ag, err := workflowagent.New("order_pipeline", []workflow.Edge{
	{From: workflow.Start, To: triageNode},
	{From: triageNode, To: fulfillNode, Route: workflow.StringRoute("fulfill")},
	{From: fulfillNode, To: notifyNode},
})'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d4a10>)])]> Where Go really stands out is in production on Cloud Run and GKE. A compiled Go agent packages onto a roughly 2 MiB Google distroless static base image , with no language VM or heavy dependency tree. A static Go binary binds to its port as soon as the container starts, skipping the module imports of dynamic languages and the JAR unpacking and JIT warmup of JVM services . After it's running, Go's concurrency model fits agent workloads naturally. Each goroutine starts with a 2 KB stack and minimal scheduling overhead , so a single instance can multiplex thousands of concurrent streaming requests and tool calls. ADK for Go also includes built-in A2A server handlers and OpenTelemetry tracing out of the box. TypeScript TypeScript erases type annotations when compiling to JavaScript, so runtime reflection isn't an option. Instead, ADK for TypeScript pairs with Zod . You pass a z.object(...) schema to the FunctionTool function, and TypeScript statically infers the parameter types of your execute callback from that single declaration: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'import { FunctionTool, LlmAgent } from "@google/adk";
import { z } from "zod";

const getWeatherTool = new FunctionTool({
 name: "get_weather",
 description: "Retrieves the current weather report for a city.",
 parameters: z.object({
 city: z.string().describe("The name of the city."),
 }),
 execute: async ({ city }) => {
 return { status: "success", report: `Sunny and 72F in ${city}.` };
 },
});

export const rootAgent = new LlmAgent({
 name: "weather_agent",
 model: "gemini-3.8-flash",
 instruction: "Answer questions about the weather in a city.",
 tools: [getWeatherTool],
});'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d5590>)])]> Your runtime input validation, the JSON Schema sent to the model, and your IDE autocomplete inside the execute callback all flow from one source of truth. TypeScript also runs on the ADK 2.0 Graph Workflow engine and ships a standalone browser bundle . Beyond Node.js services, you can run an ADK agent directly in a browser tab using WebMCP for page-level tools and Chrome's on-device Prompt API . Java ADK for Java snaps right in for teams who have standardized on Maven, Gradle, and Spring Boot. You add @Schema annotations to method parameters and assemble agents by using a fluent LlmAgent.builder() API: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'public class WeatherTools {
 public static Map<String, String> getWeather(
 @Schema(name = "city", description = "The name of the city")
 String city) {
 return Map.of("status", "success", "report", "Sunny and 72F in " + city);
 }
}

LlmAgent agent = LlmAgent.builder()
 .name("weather_agent")
 .model("gemini-3.8-flash")
 .instruction("Answer questions about the weather in a city.")
 .tools(FunctionTool.create(WeatherTools.class, "getWeather"))
 .build();'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d5550>)])]> At runtime, the runner streams agent events reactively through RxJava . For local testing and existing JVM apps, ADK for Java ships a Maven Dev UI plugin alongside first-party bridges for Spring AI and LangChain4j. Kotlin ADK for Kotlin takes a strong stance on build-time safety. Its Kotlin Symbol Processing (KSP) compiler plugin reads @Tool and @Param annotations at build time and emits the tool schema directly into bytecode: code_block <ListValue: [StructValue([('code', 'class WeatherTools {
 @Tool(
 name = "get_weather",
 description = "Retrieves the current weather report for a city.",
 )
 suspend fun getWeather(
 @Param("The name of the city.") city: String
 ): Map<String, String> = mapOf("status" to "success", "report" to "Sunny and 72F in $city")
}

val agent = LlmAgent(
 model = Gemini("gemini-3.8-flash"),
 name = "weather_agent",
 instruction = Instruction("Answer questions about the weather in a city."),
 tools = WeatherTools().generatedTools(),
)'), ('language', ''), ('caption', <wagtail.rich_text.RichText object at 0x7fc4126d7190>)])]> If a tool signature uses an unsupported type or declares a default value on a non-nullable parameter, your Gradle build fails immediately instead of breaking in production. Because the .generatedTools() function uses zero runtime reflection, it survives Android R8 shrinking . That zero-reflection design makes it possible to run the entire agent loop locally on Android. It offers JVM server support, including a Spring AI bridge and experimental ADK 2.0 graph workflows. On-device, ADK for Kotlin integrates with LiteRT-LM for tool calling and ML Kit (Gemini Nano) for text generation. It also works with Room and AppSearch for local state, and Android AppFunctions for OS-level actions, all without requiring a network connection. Choice without compromise Having five native languages in ADK means you don't have to pick a single runtime for your entire organization. Because every version of ADK speaks MCP and A2A, each component of your system can use the language that fits it best. What languages have you used to build agents with Google's ADK? Let me know what stack you're running on X , LinkedIn , or Bluesky .
